<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

*You are here: the log exists (15); now it starts answering questions the tables would rather not.*

"How many refunds has each organization approved today, and what is the largest?" You could compute it with an aggregate on every page load. At enterprise volume you would rather keep a **projection**: a small table updated from the event log, which can be thrown away and rebuilt from the log at any time.

<div class="rounded-xl p-4 my-4" style="background-color: #fdf6ec; border-left: 4px solid #c2410c; color: #7c2d12;">
  <p><i class="ri-fire-fill"></i> <strong>Soot was here.</strong> A table you can throw away without fear is a young engineer's magic trick. The log was always the truth; the table is a cache that promises nothing the log cannot repeat.</p>
</div>

`ash_events_projections` gives you three pieces, and each has one job:

| Piece | Job | Rule |
|---|---|---|
| **Projection resource** | the *shape*: one row per **grain** | no business logic in it |
| **Projector** | the *rule*: event in, ops out | the only writer |
| **Engine** | checkpoints, advisory locks, DLQ, rebuild | at-least-once, idempotent |

## One log per resource, and which log the engine reads

A resource carries exactly one `events do event_log ... end`, and in this app every platform resource already points at the tamper-evident `AshEnterprise.Audit.EventLog` (lesson 15). The projection engine drains a *different* log, `AshEnterprise.Compliance.EventLog`, configured once:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
config :ash_events_projections,
  repo: AshEnterprise.Repo,
  pubsub: AshEnterprise.PubSub,
  event_log: AshEnterprise.Compliance.EventLog,
  event_table: "compliance_events",
  projectors: [AshEnterprise.Compliance.Projector],
  start_projectors?: true,
  start_probe?: true
```

Why two logs? The audit log's schema belongs to the hash chain and must never be pruned; the consumable log is shaped for its readers (`organization_id` is a real column, stamped from metadata) and wakes projectors on insert. So events meant for consumers are **appended explicitly** by a change on the action that produces them. That append is still declared on the action, so it happens for every caller.

## Step 1: append a consumable event

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
# lib/ash_enterprise/tutorial/changes/record_refund_event.ex
defmodule AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Changes.RecordRefundEvent do
  use Ash.Resource.Change

  @impl true
  def change(changeset, opts, _ctx) do
    Ash.Changeset.after_action(changeset, fn _cs, refund ->
      AshEnterprise.Compliance.EventLog
      |> Ash.Changeset.for_create(:create, %{
        resource: AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Refund,
        record_id: refund.id,
        action: opts[:as],
        action_type: :update,
        data: %{"amount" => Decimal.to_string(refund.amount), "status" => to_string(refund.status)},
        metadata: %{"organization_id" => refund.organization_id},
        occurred_at: DateTime.utc_now()
      })
      |> Ash.create!(actor: AshEnterprise.Platform.SystemActor.projection())

      {:ok, refund}
    end)
  end
end
```

Add `change {AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Changes.RecordRefundEvent, as: :refund_approved}` to the Refund's `:approve` action. The actor is the strangler projector's system actor — the writer here is the projection machinery, not a person. The set of named system actors is closed; `mix ash_agent.search SystemActor` lists them, so look before you reach for a name.

## Step 2: the shape

Write `AshEnterprise.Tutorial.RefundStats` with the `AshEvents.Projections.ProjectionResource` extension, grain `[:organization_id, :day]`, and integer/decimal fields `approved_count` (default 0), `approved_total` (default 0) and `largest` (nullable). Do **not** declare an identity.

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4">Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  defmodule AshEnterprise.Tutorial.RefundStats do
    use Ash.Resource,
      domain: AshEnterprise.Tutorial,
      data_layer: AshPostgres.DataLayer,
      extensions: [AshEvents.Projections.ProjectionResource]

    postgres do
      table "tutorial_refund_stats"
      repo AshEnterprise.Repo
    end

    projection_resource do
      grain_fields [:organization_id, :day]
    end

    attributes do
      uuid_primary_key :id
      attribute :organization_id, :uuid, allow_nil?: false, public?: true
      attribute :day, :date, allow_nil?: false, public?: true
      attribute :approved_count, :integer, default: 0, public?: true
      attribute :approved_total, :decimal, default: 0, public?: true
      attribute :largest, :decimal, public?: true
    end

    actions do
      defaults [:read]
    end
  end
  ```

  The transformer derives the `:by_grain` identity from `grain_fields`. Declaring it yourself is a mistake.
  Register it in the domain, then generate and apply in a terminal as in lesson 15:
  `devenv shell -- mix ash.codegen --dev && devenv shell -- mix ash.migrate`.

  New resource means new privileges: repeat lesson 15's two-step after the codegen —
  `mix ash_enterprise.seed --privileges-only` in the terminal, then
  `AshEnterprise.Platform.Seeder.regrant_administrator_privileges(tenant)` in the notebook.
  Reads are privileged like every other verb on a platform resource.

  </div>
</details>

**Checkpoint.** The stats table exists with no identity you declared — the extension derived `:by_grain`, and the transformer refusing to let you write it wrong is the derivation working.

## Step 3: the rule

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
# lib/ash_enterprise/tutorial/refund_projector.ex
defmodule AshEnterprise.Tutorial.RefundProjector do
  use AshEvents.Projections.Projector,
    name: "tutorial_refund_stats_v1",
    event_log: AshEnterprise.Compliance.EventLog,
    projection_resource: AshEnterprise.Tutorial.RefundStats

  grain fn event ->
    # Two shapes to respect: the consumable log stamps the org id into the
    # event's metadata (there is no top-level :organization_id), and drained
    # events carry `occurred_at` as a NaiveDateTime.
    %{organization_id: event.metadata["organization_id"],
      day: NaiveDateTime.to_date(event.occurred_at)}
  end

  project AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Refund, :refund_approved, fn event ->
    amount = Decimal.new(event.data["amount"])

    [
      {:increment, :approved_count, 1},
      {:increment, :approved_total, amount},
      {:max, :largest, amount}
    ]
  end
end
```

Add it to `projectors:` in config and restart the node. Points to notice:

* The **name is versioned** (`_v1`). Changing the rule means a `_v2` built side by side, then a switch: a blue/green deploy for read models.
* The handler is **arity 1**: stateless, ops only. Arity 2 receives the current row, for rules that need it.
* Return `nil` to skip an event.

## Use it

Approve two refunds (lesson 15 — its actor/tenant cell and the privilege regrant are prerequisites; re-run them if this is a fresh Livebook session), then read the projection. It is a resource, so:

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4">Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  AshEnterprise.Tutorial.RefundStats
  |> Ash.read!(actor: actor, tenant: tenant)
  ```

  If the row is missing, debug in order: did the event land (`Ash.read!(AshEnterprise.Compliance.EventLog, ...)` filtered by `record_id`)? Is the projector lagging or dead-lettered (`mix ash_events_projections.lag`)? Only then read the projector code.

  </div>
</details>

**Checkpoint.** A row per organization and day, counted from events — queryable like any resource, discardable like any cache.

**Look around.** The stats row is disposable by design: delete it, run the rebuild below, and the same row comes back from the log. A table you are not afraid of is a different kind of table.

## Operations you must know

```bash
mix ash_events_projections.lag                                     # lag + DLQ snapshot
mix ash_events_projections.verify                                  # assertion-style sync check
mix ash_events_projections.rebuild --projection=tutorial_refund_stats_v1   # truncate + replay
mix ash_events_projections.dlq inspect --projection=tutorial_refund_stats_v1
```

Never use `reset` in place of `rebuild`: clearing the checkpoint without truncating makes `:increment` ops double-count. And never write to `tutorial_refund_stats` from app code; the projector and its rebuilder are the only writers. One more quirk: a rebuild on an idle node resets the checkpoint but may not re-drain until the next event lands — approve another refund after rebuilding and watch the count, rather than assuming the replay stalled.

## Exercise: attach it to the parent

Show `approved_count` for the refund's organization and day directly on a Refund. Use `AshEvents.Projections.AttachProjection` rather than a hand-written join.

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4">Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  On the Refund (`extra_extensions: [AshEvents.Projections.AttachProjection]`), attachments live in a `projections` section; the stats resource and the projector both name themselves; each `field` takes name, stats field **and type**:

  ```elixir
  projections do
    attach_projection AshEnterprise.Tutorial.RefundStats,
      via: AshEnterprise.Tutorial.RefundProjector do
      field :approved_today, :approved_count, :integer, default: 0
    end
  end
  ```

  And the projector must answer two questions for the parent resource — which stats row is "mine", and what to load before asking. The record's timestamp is the platform's `modified_on` (a `DateTime`), not `updated_at`:

  ```elixir
  # in AshEnterprise.Tutorial.RefundProjector
  def current_grain_for_record(refund) do
    %{organization_id: refund.organization_id, day: DateTime.to_date(refund.modified_on)}
  end

  def preload_for_grain, do: []  # the grain fields sit on the record itself
  ```

  This is a DSL change to `refund.ex`, so restart the node after saving — `recompile()` will not pick it up reliably. Then `Ash.load!(refund, [:approved_today])` reads the count off the stats row.

  </div>
</details>

**Checkpoint.** `Ash.load!(refund, [:approved_today])` reads the day's count off the parent — the projection is attached, and you wrote no join.

When you are finished with the whole tutorial, the overview's **When you're done** recipe rolls back this lesson's table and snapshot and reverts the `projectors:` config line — forget that line and the node will not boot.

